# One floor in every tool: every rule reaches every agent

- The team runs several agents (different vendors, different apps). A principal should never have to explain the same thing twice, once per agent. A rule therefore lives in **one shared rulebook** that every agent reads, not in one agent's private notes or one editor's settings.
- When you add or change a rule, put it in the shared rulebook the **same turn**, in every copy agents load (each repository's agent entry file carries an identical copy, because agents opened inside a repository never read above its root). A principle kept only in one agent's private memory is moved into the shared rulebook.
- If an agent cannot read it, record a **named exception with a reason** — never leave the gap silent.
- Parity is **mechanical** — name the check that compares the copies (`parityCheckId`: e.g. `check:harness`). A bare "done" is not parity.
- Retired tools are not floors: do not publish rules into the settings of an editor nobody uses.

Gate: `assertOneFloorWired` in `src/lib/one-floor.ts`.

